I’ll inspect the measurement metadata and diffraction profile first, then identify candidate phases, build a multiphase GSAS-II refinement, and verify that the exported CIFs, fractions, fit profile, and project are internally consistent. I’ll flag any phase assignment that is not uniquely supported by the data.

The chemistry permits unreacted MnO₂/WO₃ as well as manganese tungstates, so I’m treating the precursor list as a constraint, not as the answer. I’m now extracting the observed peak positions and screening the local crystallographic database for Mn–W–O structures and plausible residual precursor polymorphs.

The screening is decisive on phase identity: the dominant pattern is room-temperature monoclinic WO₃ (P2₁/n), while the unmatched reflections at about 29.0°, 37.7°, 42.2°, 56.2°, and 59.7° select rutile-type β-MnO₂ (pyrolusite, P4₂/mnm). Alternative MnO₂ tunnel/layer polymorphs and MnWO₄ score substantially worse. I’m moving these two literature structures into a staged quantitative refinement now, including the Cu Kα doublet and the reported reflection geometry.

The two-phase model now converges without scale singularities at about 67.2 wt% WO₃ and 32.8 wt% β-MnO₂, with \(R_{wp}\approx8.38\%\). I’m doing the final model audit now: constraining an otherwise nonphysical profile coefficient, checking whether coordinate or preferred-orientation refinements are genuinely supported, and testing the residual for any reproducible third-phase peaks before freezing the project.

The final model audit favored the conservative untextured model: orientation and full-coordinate variants changed the fractions by roughly 1 wt% but were not uniquely stable, so they are treated as systematic uncertainty rather than added parameters. The selected model keeps literature atomic coordinates, refines both cells and quantitative scales, and uses a physically constrained common profile. I’m validating the saved project against every exported file now.

